<ArticleLayout slug="douyin-video-specs-safe-zones" ctaLabel="Talk to the production team" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Ask for Douyin video specifications in English and every answer you get is about TikTok. The two apps share a parent company and a vertical frame. Whether they share an interface is something nobody has published a comparison of, and the safe-zone figures in circulation are almost all TikTok's.</p>

  <h2>What aspect ratio does Douyin use?</h2>

  <p>A 9:16 vertical frame at 1080 by 1920 pixels. All four sources that name a native ratio give 9:16, and all four give that size. That is the most settled row on this page. The safe zone is the least settled, and it is where the TikTok figures have filled the gap.</p>

  <h2>How to read these numbers</h2>

  <p>These specs are not read from a creator backend. Douyin's creator surfaces serve no specification text to anyone not logged in, so no open source reaches the official value for the app.</p>

  <p>What follows is the most commonly published figure for each spec, collected across 12 independent sources in Chinese and English on 10 September 2026, with the number of sources that agreed shown per row. Rows marked consensus had broad agreement. Rows marked contested did not, and those carry the full spread rather than a single number.</p>

  <p>Treat this as the best available public reading, not as the rule. Platform specs change without announcement and often without an English notice at all. Before you build a batch to any number on this page, open your own creator account and confirm it there. Reviewed 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>One exception runs through the page. Douyin's developer documentation for its upload API is publicly readable, and where a figure comes from it, the row says so and says what it covers.</p>

  <h2>The one platform text anyone can read</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Douyin's Open Platform documentation for its upload API, read on 10 September 2026, states that a video uploaded through that API may total up to 4GB, must be sent in chunks above 300MB, is best sent as MP4 or WebM, and may not run past 15 minutes. That documentation governs apps publishing through the API. It does not say the phone app or the web creator platform share those limits, and no source found says they do.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two Douyin Open Platform documentation pages fetched unauthenticated on 10 September 2026, text read directly. Primary reading for the API route only, not a statement of in-app limits.</p>

  <p>That is a real platform statement, and it is narrower than it looks. It tells a developer what the upload API accepts. It says nothing about what a creator posting from the phone can upload, and treating it as app guidance is exactly how one published page ended up repeating API limits as creator advice.</p>

  <h2>Resolution, duration, codec and file size</h2>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The most commonly published Douyin post format is a 9:16 vertical frame at 1080x1920 pixels. Four of four independent sources that name a native ratio give 9:16, and all four give 1080x1920.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 12 independent published references, 11 Chinese-language and 1 English-language, collected 10 September 2026, counted per domain and reported as the most commonly published figure. Modal value, not a primary reading.</p>

