  <p>Both boxes are derivations from published numbers, not measurements of either app. If one master has to run on both platforms, design to the two-app box and you are clear of every figure anyone has printed.</p>

  <h2>Where do Douyin captions and end cards get covered?</h2>

  <p>On the published numbers, lower than a TikTok layout assumes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On the published figures, a caption placed just above a 270-pixel TikTok bottom inset sits about 110 pixels inside the 380-pixel bottom band that one Chinese source gives for Douyin. That is a comparison of two sets of published numbers, not a measurement of either app.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on published TikTok and Douyin inset figures collected 10 September 2026. Derived, contested on both inputs.</p>

  <p>End cards follow the same logic. A call to action placed in the lower fifth of a TikTok cut sits inside the largest published Douyin bottom band. Lift it into the two-app box before re-exporting.</p>

  <p>On sound, none of the twelve Douyin sources publishes an assumption about whether viewers watch with sound on, so this page has no figure to give. Burned-in captions inside the box cover both cases, which is a production choice rather than a platform rule.</p>

  <h2>Re-exporting a TikTok master for Douyin</h2>

  <p>A checklist built from the figures above. Every line is a derivation from published numbers or a production choice, not a platform instruction.</p>

